	<h3>How to build a mathematical model using only MaBoSS core C++ </h3>
	
	  If you wish to build your logical model without the environment, here are the steps on how to proceed.
<ol>
<!--	  <li>Install MaBoSS on your desktop (we advise to create a directory where the models and the program will be stored)</li> -->
	  <li class="vspace">Install MaBoSS as described in the <a href="#" class="clean-link" onclick="show('installation');return false;">installation page</a></li>
	  <li class="vspace">Create the model using MaBoSS language in a file (<span class="tt">myfile.bnd</span> for instance):
	    <ul>
	      <li>write the logic for each node</li>
	      <li>assign parameter values to each transition rate</li>
	    </ul>
	  </li>
	  <li class="vspace">Create the configuration file (<span class="tt">myfile.cfg</span> for instance) to define the simulation parameters</li>
	  <li class="vspace">Run MaBoSS (the order of the arguments does not matter):
	    <ul>
	      <!--	      <li>open a terminal</li> -->
	      <!-- 	      <li>go to the MaBoSS directory created in 1.</li> -->
	      <li style="margin-top: 5px;" class="vspace">Type:
		<br>
		<span class="tt">MaBoSS -c myfile.cfg -o myfile_out myfile.bnd</span>
		<br>(we assume that MaBoSS is accessible through you PATH)</li>
	      <li class="vspace">MaBoSS creates four output files:
		<ul>
		  <li><span class="tt">file_out_proptraj.csv</span>
		    <br>This file contains the network state probabilities on a time window, the entropy, the transition
		    entropy and the Hamming distance distribution</li>
